      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">What is the difference between a car loan and a chattel mortgage in Australia?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">A chattel mortgage is a business finance product where the business owns the vehicle from day one but the lender holds a mortgage over it as security. It is typically used by ABN holders and can offer GST and depreciation benefits. A standard car loan is a personal finance product. If you are buying a vehicle for business use, a chattel mortgage is usually the more tax-efficient structure.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Should I use my savings or redraw from my home loan to buy a car?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Redrawing from your home loan converts equity-building debt into a non-deductible personal expense and typically extends your home loan term. While your home loan rate may be lower than a car loan rate, you could end up paying interest on the car for 20+ years if you simply add it to the mortgage. If you do redraw, set up a separate split with a distinct repayment plan to clear it in three to five years.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

